=========\par
\par
Process jobs asynchronously with Batch API.\par
\par
Learn how to use OpenAI's Batch API to send asynchronous groups of requests with 50% lower costs, a separate pool of significantly higher rate limits, and a clear 24-hour turnaround time. The service is ideal for processing jobs that don't require immediate responses. You can also [explore the API reference directly here](/docs/api-reference/batch).\par
\par
Overview\par
--------\par
\par
While some uses of the OpenAI Platform require you to send synchronous requests, there are many cases where requests do not need an immediate response or [rate limits](/docs/guides/rate-limits) prevent you from executing a large number of queries quickly. Batch processing jobs are often helpful in use cases like:\par
\par
1.  Running evaluations\par
2.  Classifying large datasets\par
3.  Embedding content repositories\par
\par
The Batch API offers a straightforward set of endpoints that allow you to collect a set of requests into a single file, kick off a batch processing job to execute these requests, query for the status of that batch while the underlying requests execute, and eventually retrieve the collected results when the batch is complete.\par
\par
Compared to using standard endpoints directly, Batch API has:\par
\par
1.  **Better cost efficiency:** 50% cost discount compared to synchronous APIs\par
2.  **Higher rate limits:** [Substantially more headroom](/settings/organization/limits) compared to the synchronous APIs\par
3.  **Fast completion times:** Each batch completes within 24 hours (and often more quickly)\par
\par
Getting started\par
---------------\par
\par
### 1\\. Prepare your batch file\par
\par
Batches start with a `.jsonl` file where each line contains the details of an individual request to the API. For now, the available endpoints are `/v1/responses` ([Responses API](/docs/api-reference/responses)), `/v1/chat/completions` ([Chat Completions API](/docs/api-reference/chat)), `/v1/embeddings` ([Embeddings API](/docs/api-reference/embeddings)), and '/v1/completions' ([Completions API](/docs/api-reference/completions)). For a given input file, the parameters in each line's `body` field are the same as the parameters for the underlying endpoint. Each request must include a unique `custom_id` value, which you can use to reference results after completion. Here's an example of an input file with 2 requests. Note that each input file can only include requests to a single model.\par
\par
```jsonl\par
\{"custom_id": "request-1", "method": "POST", "url": "/v1/chat/completions", "body": \{"model": "gpt-3.5-turbo-0125", "messages": [\{"role": "system", "content": "You are a helpful assistant."\},\{"role": "user", "content": "Hello world!"\}],"max_tokens": 1000\}\}\par
\{"custom_id": "request-2", "method": "POST", "url": "/v1/chat/completions", "body": \{"model": "gpt-3.5-turbo-0125", "messages": [\{"role": "system", "content": "You are an unhelpful assistant."\},\{"role": "user", "content": "Hello world!"\}],"max_tokens": 1000\}\}\par
```\par
\par
### 2\\. Upload your batch input file\par
\par
Similar to our [Fine-tuning API](/docs/guides/fine-tuning), you must first upload your input file so that you can reference it correctly when kicking off batches. Upload your `.jsonl` file using the [Files API](/docs/api-reference/files).\par
\par
Upload files for Batch API\par
\par
```javascript\par
import fs from "fs";\par
import OpenAI from "openai";\par
const openai = new OpenAI();\par
\par
const file = await openai.files.create(\{\par
  file: fs.createReadStream("batchinput.jsonl"),\par
  purpose: "batch",\par
\});\par
\par
console.log(file);\par
```\par
\par
```python\par
from openai import OpenAI\par
client = OpenAI()\par
\par
batch_input_file = client.files.create(\par
    file=open("batchinput.jsonl", "rb"),\par
    purpose="batch"\par
)\par
\par
print(batch_input_file)\par
```\par
\par
```bash\par
curl https://api.openai.com/v1/files \\\par
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $OPENAI_API_KEY" \\\par
  -F purpose="batch" \\\par
  -F file="@batchinput.jsonl"\par
```\par
\par
### 3\\. Create the batch\par
\par
Once you've successfully uploaded your input file, you can use the input File object's ID to create a batch. In this case, let's assume the file ID is `file-abc123`. For now, the completion window can only be set to `24h`. You can also provide custom metadata via an optional `metadata` parameter.\par
\par
Create the Batch\par
\par
```javascript\par
import OpenAI from "openai";\par
const openai = new OpenAI();\par
\par
const batch = await openai.batches.create(\{\par
  input_file_id: "file-abc123",\par
  endpoint: "/v1/chat/completions",\par
  completion_window: "24h"\par
\});\par
\par
console.log(batch);\par
```\par
\par
```python\par
from openai import OpenAI\par
client = OpenAI()\par
\par
batch_input_file_id = batch_input_file.id\par
client.batches.create(\par
    input_file_id=batch_input_file_id,\par
    endpoint="/v1/chat/completions",\par
    completion_window="24h",\par
    metadata=\{\par
        "description": "nightly eval job"\par
    \}\par
)\par
```\par
\par
```bash\par
curl https://api.openai.com/v1/batches \\\par
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $OPENAI_API_KEY" \\\par
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \\\par
  -d '\{\par
    "input_file_id": "file-abc123",\par
    "endpoint": "/v1/chat/completions",\par
    "completion_window": "24h"\par
  \}'\par
```\par
\par
This request will return a [Batch object](/docs/api-reference/batch/object) with metadata about your batch:\par
\par
```python\par
\{\par
  "id": "batch_abc123",\par
  "object": "batch",\par
  "endpoint": "/v1/chat/completions",\par
  "errors": null,\par
  "input_file_id": "file-abc123",\par
  "completion_window": "24h",\par
  "status": "validating",\par
  "output_file_id": null,\par
  "error_file_id": null,\par
  "created_at": 1714508499,\par
  "in_progress_at": null,\par
  "expires_at": 1714536634,\par
  "completed_at": null,\par
  "failed_at": null,\par
  "expired_at": null,\par
  "request_counts": \{\par
    "total": 0,\par
    "completed": 0,\par
    "failed": 0\par
  \},\par
  "metadata": null\par
\}\par
```\par
\par
### 4\\. Check the status of a batch\par
\par
You can check the status of a batch at any time, which will also return a Batch object.\par
\par
Check the status of a batch\par
\par
```javascript\par
import OpenAI from "openai";\par
const openai = new OpenAI();\par
\par
const batch = await openai.batches.retrieve("batch_abc123");\par
console.log(batch);\par
```\par
\par
```python\par
from openai import OpenAI\par
client = OpenAI()\par
\par
const batch = client.batches.retrieve("batch_abc123")\par
print(batch)\par
```\par
\par
```bash\par
curl https://api.openai.com/v1/batches/batch_abc123 \\\par
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $OPENAI_API_KEY" \\\par
  -H "Content-Type: application/json"\par
```\par
\par
The status of a given Batch object can be any of the following:\par
\par
|Status|Description|\par
|---|---|\par
|validating|the input file is being validated before the batch can begin|\par
|failed|the input file has failed the validation process|\par
|in_progress|the input file was successfully validated and the batch is currently being run|\par
|finalizing|the batch has completed and the results are being prepared|\par
|completed|the batch has been completed and the results are ready|\par
|expired|the batch was not able to be completed within the 24-hour time window|\par
|cancelling|the batch is being cancelled (may take up to 10 minutes)|\par
|cancelled|the batch was cancelled|\par
\par
### 5\\. Retrieve the results\par
\par
Once the batch is complete, you can download the output by making a request against the [Files API](/docs/api-reference/files) via the `output_file_id` field from the Batch object and writing it to a file on your machine, in this case `batch_output.jsonl`\par
\par
Retrieving the batch results\par
\par
```javascript\par
import OpenAI from "openai";\par
const openai = new OpenAI();\par
\par
const fileResponse = await openai.files.content("file-xyz123");\par
const fileContents = await fileResponse.text();\par
\par
console.log(fileContents);\par
```\par
\par
```python\par
from openai import OpenAI\par
client = OpenAI()\par
\par
file_response = client.files.content("file-xyz123")\par
print(file_response.text)\par
```\par
\par
```bash\par
curl https://api.openai.com/v1/files/file-xyz123/content \\\par
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $OPENAI_API_KEY" > batch_output.jsonl\par
```\par
\par
The output `.jsonl` file will have one response line for every successful request line in the input file. Any failed requests in the batch will have their error information written to an error file that can be found via the batch's `error_file_id`.\par
\par
Note that the output line order **may not match** the input line order. Instead of relying on order to process your results, use the custom\\_id field which will be present in each line of your output file and allow you to map requests in your input to results in your output.\par
\par
```jsonl\par
\{"id": "batch_req_123", "custom_id": "request-2", "response": \{"status_code": 200, "request_id": "req_123", "body": \{"id": "chatcmpl-123", "object": "chat.completion", "created": 1711652795, "model": "gpt-3.5-turbo-0125", "choices": [\{"index": 0, "message": \{"role": "assistant", "content": "Hello."\}, "logprobs": null, "finish_reason": "stop"\}], "usage": \{"prompt_tokens": 22, "completion_tokens": 2, "total_tokens": 24\}, "system_fingerprint": "fp_123"\}\}, "error": null\}\par
\{"id": "batch_req_456", "custom_id": "request-1", "response": \{"status_code": 200, "request_id": "req_789", "body": \{"id": "chatcmpl-abc", "object": "chat.completion", "created": 1711652789, "model": "gpt-3.5-turbo-0125", "choices": [\{"index": 0, "message": \{"role": "assistant", "content": "Hello! How can I assist you today?"\}, "logprobs": null, "finish_reason": "stop"\}], "usage": \{"prompt_tokens": 20, "completion_tokens": 9, "total_tokens": 29\}, "system_fingerprint": "fp_3ba"\}\}, "error": null\}\par
```\par
\par
### 6\\. Cancel a batch\par
\par
If necessary, you can cancel an ongoing batch. The batch's status will change to `cancelling` until in-flight requests are complete (up to 10 minutes), after which the status will change to `cancelled`.\par
\par
Cancelling a batch\par
\par
```javascript\par
import OpenAI from "openai";\par
const openai = new OpenAI();\par
\par
const batch = await openai.batches.cancel("batch_abc123");\par
console.log(batch);\par
```\par
\par
```python\par
from openai import OpenAI\par
client = OpenAI()\par
\par
client.batches.cancel("batch_abc123")\par
```\par
\par
```bash\par
curl https://api.openai.com/v1/batches/batch_abc123/cancel \\\par
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $OPENAI_API_KEY" \\\par
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \\\par
  -X POST\par
```\par
